# Deploy settings for _build/deploy.sh. Copy this file to _build/deploy.env,
# fill in the site's values, delete what the site doesn't use, and commit it.
#
# deploy.env is committed, so it holds only non-secret values. Personal or secret
# values (SSH users) go in .env next to _build/, which overrides this file. The
# environment overrides both. Values are read literally: no $VARS, no command
# substitution. Quotes around a whole value are stripped.
#
# Paths below are relative to the folder above _build/ unless noted.

# --- Host and layout ----------------------------------------------------------

# Host for the bare targets: `deploy.sh staging` deploys to <host>-staging.
# cloudways | wpengine | kinsta. Host-prefixed targets work without it.
DEPLOY_HOST=

# The WordPress folder (the one holding wp-content/). Default: the folder above
# _build/, which suits both wordpress/_build/ and a root _build/ with WordPress
# at the repo root. Set it only when _build/ sits outside the WordPress folder,
# e.g. `wordpress` for _build/ at the repo root next to wordpress/.
# The script derives the push from the layout: when WordPress is in a repo
# subfolder, WP Engine and Kinsta get a commit of that folder alone and the
# Cloudways hook extracts only that folder; at the repo root the whole packaged
# tree is pushed.
# DEPLOY_WORDPRESS_DIR=.

# Branch the host installs (default master). Must match the Kinsta hook's branch.
DEPLOY_REMOTE_BRANCH=master

# --- Build --------------------------------------------------------------------

# Shell command run before packaging. Empty means no build step (the script says so).
# Examples: pnpm run build | npx gulp release | cd wp-content/themes/<theme> && yarn build:production
DEPLOY_BUILD_TASK=

# Folder the build runs in (default .). The clean-tree check covers it too.
# Use `..` when wordpress/_build/ builds from a package.json at the repo root.
# DEPLOY_BUILD_DIR=.

# --- Payload ------------------------------------------------------------------

# Ignored build output to add, and tracked paths to leave out. One entry per line,
# relative to the WordPress folder; blank lines and # comments are skipped.
DEPLOY_INCLUDE_FILE=_build/.deploy_include.txt
DEPLOY_EXCLUDE_FILE=_build/.deploy_exclude.txt

# Extended regexes over paths relative to the WordPress folder. The defaults in
# deploy.sh suit most sites; override only with a reason.
# Paths that fail the deploy if they are in the payload (default: .env*, wp-config.php, uploads).
# DEPLOY_FORBIDDEN_PAYLOAD_REGEX=
# Server paths that deploys never delete and the audit expects (default: wp-config.php,
# .htaccess, uploads, caches, plus Kinsta's and WP Engine's platform files on those hosts).
# Setting this replaces the host additions too.
# DEPLOY_SERVER_MANAGED_REGEX=

# How often to check the server's files after a push, and the wait between checks
# in seconds. Defaults: 6 attempts on WP Engine, 1 elsewhere; 10 seconds.
# DEPLOY_VERIFY_ATTEMPTS=
# DEPLOY_VERIFY_WAIT=

# Where `deploy.sh prune <target> --delete` writes its backup tarball of the files
# it removes (default: ~/wp-deploy-backups). Relative paths are under the folder
# above _build/. Keep it out of the repo: an untracked tarball blocks deploys.
# DEPLOY_PRUNE_BACKUP_DIR=

# Each deploy commit builds on the server's previous one, so the host's Git repo
# keeps every payload deployed since. Set to true, usually for one run
# (DEPLOY_NEW_HISTORY=true bash _build/deploy.sh <target>), to push a commit with
# no parent instead, e.g. after removing a secret that a payload once held.
# DEPLOY_NEW_HISTORY=

# --- Cloudways (DEPLOY_HOST=cloudways) ----------------------------------------
# Targets: cloudways-production, cloudways-staging. Run `deploy.sh install-hook
# <target>` once per environment, and again whenever deploy.sh says the hook differs.
# The SSH user is personal: put COMPOSE_CLOUDWAYS_<ENV>_SSH_USER in .env.
# COMPOSE_CLOUDWAYS_PRODUCTION_SSH_USER=
COMPOSE_CLOUDWAYS_PRODUCTION_SERVER_IP=
# Application's private_html folder; the bare repo is <GIT_PATH>/<PROJECT_REPO>.git.
# Cloudways shows it in two forms; either works:
#   /home/master/applications/<app>/private_html
#   /home/<server-id>.cloudwaysapps.com/<app>/private_html
COMPOSE_CLOUDWAYS_PRODUCTION_GIT_PATH=/home/master/applications/<app>/private_html
COMPOSE_CLOUDWAYS_PRODUCTION_PROJECT_REPO=
# Webroot (default: <GIT_PATH>/../public_html).
# COMPOSE_CLOUDWAYS_PRODUCTION_WEBROOT=

# COMPOSE_CLOUDWAYS_STAGING_SSH_USER=
COMPOSE_CLOUDWAYS_STAGING_SERVER_IP=
# Or /home/<server-id>.cloudwaysapps.com/<app>/private_html, as above.
COMPOSE_CLOUDWAYS_STAGING_GIT_PATH=/home/master/applications/<app>/private_html
COMPOSE_CLOUDWAYS_STAGING_PROJECT_REPO=
# COMPOSE_CLOUDWAYS_STAGING_WEBROOT=

# --- Pulls (pull.sh) ---------------------------------------------------------
# `pull.sh export <target>` and `pull.sh media <target>` read the Cloudways
# settings above: the SSH user and server IP, and GIT_PATH (or WEBROOT) to find
# the app's webroot. They need no PROJECT_REPO. Cloudways only, for now.
#
# Tables `export` leaves out of the dump, comma-separated, with the table prefix,
# such as large log tables. SQL_EXCLUDED_TABLES, the older per-site scripts'
# name, is read when this is unset.
# PULL_EXCLUDED_TABLES=
# SQL_EXCLUDED_TABLES=
# Where `export` writes dumps and `import` finds them (default _data). Must be
# gitignored; pull.sh refuses to write to it otherwise.
# PULL_DATA_DIR=_data
# Where `media` copies uploads to, relative to the WordPress folder (default
# wp-content/uploads). Must be gitignored.
# PULL_UPLOADS_DIR=wp-content/uploads
# `import` loads the newest dump into this Docker Compose service's database,
# then applies <PULL_DATA_DIR>/local.sql if it exists. Defaults: the compose file
# in the folder above _build/, service db, database, user and password wordpress.
# PULL_COMPOSE_DIR=.
# PULL_DB_SERVICE=db
# PULL_DB_NAME=wordpress
# PULL_DB_USER=wordpress
# PULL_DB_PASSWORD=wordpress
# Shell command `import` runs afterwards from the folder above _build/, such as a
# script that runs `wp search-replace` to point the site's URLs at local Docker.
# PULL_IMPORT_TASK=

# --- WP Engine (DEPLOY_HOST=wpengine) -----------------------------------------
# Targets: wpe-production, wpe-staging, wpe-development. Each value is that
# environment's install name; installs may have unrelated names. The Git remote is
# git@git.wpengine.com:<install>.git and SSH is <install>@<install>.ssh.wpengine.net.
# Git Push access and SSH gateway access are separate grants in the WP Engine portal.
COMPOSE_WPE_PRODUCTION=
COMPOSE_WPE_STAGING=
# COMPOSE_WPE_DEVELOPMENT=
# Webroots (default: /home/wpe-user/sites/<install>).
# COMPOSE_WPE_PRODUCTION_WEBROOT=
# COMPOSE_WPE_STAGING_WEBROOT=
# COMPOSE_WPE_DEVELOPMENT_WEBROOT=

# --- Kinsta (DEPLOY_HOST=kinsta) ----------------------------------------------
# Targets: kinsta-production, kinsta-staging. Values from MyKinsta > Info > SFTP/SSH.
# The bare repo is /www/<PROJECT_FOLDER>/private/<PROJECT_REPO>.git and its
# post-receive hook runs `git checkout -f <DEPLOY_REMOTE_BRANCH>` into the webroot.
COMPOSE_KINSTA_PROJECT_FOLDER=
COMPOSE_KINSTA_PROJECT_REPO=
# SSH login (user@host) and port, per environment.
COMPOSE_KINSTA_PRODUCTION=
COMPOSE_KINSTA_PRODUCTION_PORT=
COMPOSE_KINSTA_STAGING=
COMPOSE_KINSTA_STAGING_PORT=
# Webroots (default: /www/<PROJECT_FOLDER>/public).
# COMPOSE_KINSTA_PRODUCTION_WEBROOT=
# COMPOSE_KINSTA_STAGING_WEBROOT=
